---
import SiteLayout from "../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import LaunchHeader from "../../../components/LaunchHeader.astro";
import "../../../styles/interview.css";

const heroImage = "https://static.wixstatic.com/media/799cff_a80ba2d281d5485686186599e22cce6b~mv2.png/v1/fill/w_980%2Ch_1082%2Cal_c%2Cq_90%2Cusm_0.66_1.00_0.01%2Cenc_avif%2Cquality_auto/Screen%20Shot%202024-03-03%20at%204_24_49%20PM.png";
const processImage = "https://static.wixstatic.com/media/799cff_d1963d2ed30241c7930e816c02285912~mv2.jpg/v1/fill/w_980%2Ch_1322%2Cal_c%2Cq_85%2Cusm_0.66_1.00_0.01%2Cenc_avif%2Cquality_auto/799cff_d1963d2ed30241c7930e816c02285912~mv2.jpg";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Artist Spotlight: Interview with TerrorFoxes — DragonsRitual"
  description="DragonsRitual Artist Spotlight interview with TerrorFoxes on fantasy illustration, comics, creative process, influences and persistence."
>
  <LaunchHeader />

  <main class="interview-page">
    <article class="interview-shell">
      <header class="interview-hero">
        <div class="interview-kicker">
          <span>DRAGONSRITUAL ARCHIVE</span>
          <strong>ARTIST SPOTLIGHT // INTERVIEW</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="interview-hero__grid">
          <div class="interview-hero__copy">
            <p class="interview-overline">FEATURED ARTIST</p>
            <h1>Artist Spotlight:<br /><em>TerrorFoxes</em></h1>
            <p class="interview-deck">
              A conversation about fantasy illustration, old Fighting Fantasy gamebooks,
              comics, instinct, discipline, and the long road toward making art your life.
            </p>

            <div class="interview-meta">
              <span>INTERVIEW BY DRAGON</span>
              <span>FEBRUARY 4, 2024</span>
              <span>ARCHIVED SEPTEMBER 26, 2024</span>
            </div>

            <div class="interview-actions">
              <a href="https://www.instagram.com/terrorfoxes/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FOLLOW TERRORFOXES ↗</a>
              <a href="https://www.webtoons.com/en/canvas/simeon-grigzy-for-hire/list?title_no=751650" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">READ ON WEBTOON ↗</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="interview-hero__art">
            <img src={heroImage} alt="Artwork featured in the TerrorFoxes artist spotlight" />
            <figcaption>TERRORFOXES // ARTIST SPOTLIGHT</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="interview-body">
        <aside class="interview-index" aria-label="Interview sections">
          <span>FIELD INDEX</span>
          <a href="#introduction">INTRODUCTION</a>
          <a href="#early-days">EARLY DAYS</a>
          <a href="#influences">INFLUENCES</a>
          <a href="#process">PROCESS</a>
          <a href="#practice">PRACTICE</a>
          <a href="#comics">COMICS</a>
          <a href="#design">CHARACTER DESIGN</a>
          <a href="#goals">GOALS</a>
        </aside>

        <div class="interview-copy">
          <section id="introduction" class="interview-section interview-intro">
            <p class="interview-section__label">INTRODUCTION</p>
            <p class="interview-lead">
              In the vibrant world of art and illustration, few creators manage to capture
              the imagination like TerrorFoxes. Known for a blend of fantasy, horror and
              science fiction, he has built his work around comics, illustration and a
              persistent need to keep drawing.
            </p>
            <p>
              In this interview, DragonsRitual looks at the process, influences and
              ambitions behind that work — from childhood gamebooks and 2000AD to
              Webtoon Canvas and the dream of holding a self-published comic in hand.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="early-days" class="interview-section">
            <p class="interview-section__label">THE EARLY DAYS // FINDING PASSION IN ILLUSTRATION</p>
            <h2>How did you begin your career in the field of art, and how did you find your passion in illustration?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                I've drawn for as long as I can remember. I was that archetypal kid who
                zoned out in class, preferring to doodle in the back of my schoolbooks!
                Unfortunately, it was never encouraged—in fact, quite the opposite—so I
                simply stopped drawing in my mid-teens and didn’t pick it back up until
                years later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="influences" class="interview-section">
            <p class="interview-section__label">INSPIRATION AND INFLUENCES</p>
            <h2>Do you have favorite artists who helped shape or motivate your work?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                As a kid, the art I was most attracted to were the illustrations in the
                old Fighting Fantasy gamebooks.
              </p>
              <p>
                I didn't know the artists' names at the time, but I've since learned that
                most of my favourite pics were drawn by Iain McCaig! The only time I
                remember actually learning an artist's name because I was so impressed
                with their work was when my friend at school lent me some 2000AD comics,
                and I was exposed to Simon Bisley’s ABC Warriors art. I remember just
                being stunned by how cool his designs were, and he remains a favourite.
              </p>
              <p>
                Since then, I’ve gravitated toward some classics like Frank Frazetta and
                Bernie Wrightson. I’m always picking up new inspirations!
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <figure class="interview-break-art">
            <img src={processImage} alt="TerrorFoxes artwork from the original DragonsRitual interview" loading="lazy" />
            <figcaption>FROM THE ORIGINAL DRAGONSRITUAL ARTIST SPOTLIGHT</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <section id="process" class="interview-section">
            <p class="interview-section__label">CREATIVE PROCESS // INTUITION VS. PLANNING</p>
            <h2>What do you feel when you put the pencil to paper? Is it intuitive, or do you have to really think?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                Most of my ballpoint work is done intuitively. I often just put the pen to
                paper, make a shape, and see where it goes from there. I enjoy the
                challenge of creating something aesthetically pleasing from an initially
                random design.
              </p>
              <p>
                Occasionally, I do have a specific motivation for a drawing when I get an
                idea I just have to put down. In both cases, I feel freedom and joy—
                especially during the first few strokes as the drawing comes together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <aside class="interview-pull">
            <span>FROM THE INTERVIEW</span>
            <blockquote>
              “I often just put the pen to paper, make a shape, and see where it goes from there.”
            </blockquote>
          </aside>

          <section id="practice" class="interview-section">
            <p class="interview-section__label">ADVICE FOR ARTISTS // PRACTICE AND PERSISTENCE</p>
            <h2>What advice would you give to artists, new and veteran?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                I don't feel comfortable giving out advice, as I'm still a beginner.
                However, I can share what has worked for me: I had little improvement
                until I bought a pocket-sized sketchbook and resolved to draw every day.
                I started filling the pages whenever I could, even in short ten-minute
                bursts.
              </p>
              <p>
                I then posted the results on Instagram, which helped me maintain a routine
                and provided motivation. So, I’d say: put the time in, draw, and practise
                as much as you can—while not being too disheartened by slow progress.
              </p>
              <p>
                That said, while social media is useful and fun, I've recently found
                myself spending more time on it than I'd like. Checking up on Instagram,
                TikTok, Threads, X, and DeviantArt takes more time than I want, so I plan
                to step back and focus more on my comics and practice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>Do you have any dream jobs?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>Any job where I can be paid to draw fantasy/horror/sci-fi ideas would be a dream!</p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="comics" class="interview-section">
            <p class="interview-section__label">COMICS // WEBTOON // LEARNING IN PUBLIC</p>
            <h2>You have two fantasy-themed comics on the web. How is the experience?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                Yes, I have two fantasy-themed comics on Webtoon Canvas: Simeon & Grigzy
                For Hire! and Dunjun Gremz. I'm enjoying both, and I find Webtoons very
                user-friendly for uploading episodes. The experience has been positive
                overall. My main issue is not being able to release issues faster!
              </p>
              <p>
                Comics take ages to draw, letter, and edit, especially with my detailed
                art style, which is why Simeon & Grigzy has transitioned from full colour
                to just black and white.
              </p>
              <p>
                I also need to practise colouring, so I created Dunjun Gremz as a smaller
                comic to keep me on track with that.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>Were the stories pre-planned, or are you making them up as you go?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                My comics are pre-planned but only as a loose skeleton. I have the story
                mapped out, but I’m fleshing out a lot of the details as I go. Dunjun
                Gremz is usually a one-joke strip though that I make up as I go.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="design" class="interview-section">
            <p class="interview-section__label">CHARACTER DESIGN AND ADAPTABILITY</p>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                Both characters in Simeon & Grigzy were created with adaptability in
                mind. They are quite standard-looking—one’s basically a ninja, and the
                other a generic ogre/orc. This allows me to tinker with their designs over
                time. I expect their appearances to change significantly from their
                original looks in a couple of years while still maintaining the
                ninja/ogre template.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>What has comic creation taught you?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                What I’ve learned is that comics are incredibly time-consuming, and each
                aspect—pencilling, inking, colouring, and lettering—is a discipline in
                itself that could take a lifetime to master. Writing, too! Managing all
                these tasks while trying to learn is far more challenging than anyone who
                hasn’t drawn their own comic might appreciate.
              </p>
              <p>
                One frustrating limitation has been needing to tone down the violence for
                Simeon & Grigzy. Originally, it was meant to be hyper-violent, but
                Webtoons has guidelines against that, so I’ve had to get creative and hide
                some content or imply violence happening off-camera. There are plenty of
                panels on the cutting room floor!
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>What would you like readers to know about you?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                I've had no formal art training, so all my bad habits are mine alone! I do
                try to watch the occasional YouTube art tutorial video, but only in small
                doses as I'd much rather be spending my time drawing instead!
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="goals" class="interview-section">
            <p class="interview-section__label">OTHER PASSIONS AND LIFE GOALS</p>
            <h2>Do you have other passions or life goals that flow with your creative side?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                I find now that almost all of my free time is taken up with drawing, which
                is fine with me, as it’s something I stopped doing while still at school
                and wish I’d stuck with. Instead, I wasted a lot of years not doing
                something that gives me a lot of pleasure and satisfaction.
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>Where do you see yourself in the years to come?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                I hope to be a lot more polished and professional in years to come. My
                only goal for now is to keep drawing and improving while continuing my
                comics. Although, I’d like to self-publish my own printed comic book one
                day, just for the thrill of seeing it and holding it in my hand!
              </p>
            </div>

            <h2>Is illustration your professional career of choice or more of a hobby?</h2>
            <div class="interview-answer">
              <p>
                It's a hobby for now. But it'd be nice to be professional enough to
                support myself with my art one day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <footer class="interview-end">
            <span>END OF INTERVIEW // ARCHIVE SIGNAL REMAINS OPEN</span>
            <h2>Keep drawing. Keep building.</h2>
            <p>
              TerrorFoxes continues to develop his comics and illustration work. Visit
              his current pages below, or return to the DragonsRitual front page.
            </p>
            <div>
              <a href="https://www.instagram.com/terrorfoxes/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">INSTAGRAM ↗</a>
              <a href="https://www.webtoons.com/en/canvas/simeon-grigzy-for-hire/list?title_no=751650" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WEBTOON ↗</a>
              <a href="/">RETURN TO DAILY →</a>
            </div>
          </footer>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
